= What Planning Actually Is

Before a single worker is hired, rupee spent or machine bought, someone decides
*what* the firm is trying to achieve, *how* it will get there, *by when*, and
*who* will do each part. That deciding-in-advance is planning — the function
every other function builds on, because you cannot organise people around a
task that has not been defined, staff a structure that does not exist, or
control performance against a standard that was never set.

- *Definition:* planning means *setting objectives in advance and developing
  the courses of action* to achieve them — deciding in advance what to do, how
  to do it, when to do it and who is to do it.
- *A bridge:* planning bridges the gap between *where we stand now* and *where
  we want to reach*.
- *A mental exercise:* it requires applying the mind — foreseeing, imagining,
  judging — before any physical action follows.
- *A function of choice:* planning always involves *decision-making* — if
  there is only one possible course of action, there is nothing to plan.

== The features of planning

- *Planning focuses on achieving objectives:* every plan serves stated goals —
  without objectives, plans have nothing to aim at.
- *Planning is a primary function:* it comes *first* — organising, staffing,
  directing and controlling all follow the plan. It precedes the execution of
  every other function.
- *Planning is pervasive:* every level and every department plans — the top
  plans strategy, the middle plans programmes, the supervisor plans the week.
- *Planning is continuous:* plans are made, revised and remade — one plan's
  end is the next plan's beginning.
- *Planning is futuristic:* it works with *forecasts* — it prepares for
  tomorrow by studying today's trends.
- *Planning involves decision-making:* the planner *chooses* among
  alternatives — that choice is the heart of the function.
- *Planning is a mental exercise:* it is thinking before doing — imagination,
  judgment and foresight on paper first.

== Why planning matters — the importance

- *Provides direction:* the plan tells every department and employee *where to
  act and what to aim at* — efforts align instead of scattering.
- *Reduces the risk of uncertainty:* by anticipating change, the planner sees
  troubles early and prepares responses rather than improvising.
- *Reduces overlapping and wasteful activities:* a clear plan coordinates
  departments, so two teams stop buying the same material or mailing the same
  customer twice.
- *Promotes innovation:* planning forces managers to look for *new and better*
  ways of doing things — new products, new methods, new markets.
- *Facilitates decision-making:* with objectives and alternatives already on
  the table, decisions become rational choices rather than guesses.
- *Establishes standards for controlling:* the plan fixes the targets against
  which *controlling* later compares actual performance — without planning,
  controlling has no benchmark.

== The limitations — what planning cannot do

- *Leads to rigidity:* managers follow the written plan even when the
  situation has changed and flexibility would pay better.
- *May not work in a dynamic environment:* the future resists prediction —
  technology, tastes and laws change faster than plans can be redrawn.
- *Reduces creativity:* with everything decided in advance, middle and lower
  levels simply *follow* rather than think and suggest.
- *Involves huge cost:* expert advice, research, data collection, meetings —
  planning spends real money before any real action begins.
- *Is time-consuming:* collecting facts, weighing options and drafting plans
  delays decisions — and sometimes the delay itself loses the opportunity.
- *Does not guarantee success:* a plan is only a road map — the environment
  can still turn, and poor execution can waste a brilliant plan.

= The Planning Process

The syllabus asks for the planning *process* — the sequence of steps a firm
follows from the first spark of a goal to the moment results are checked.
Questions describe one step in words ("the managers listed four ways to raise
funds, then compared their costs") and ask you to name it, so the *order* and
the *one-line job* of each step both matter.

#figure(image("assets/bs_planning_process.png", width: 100%), caption: [Figure 1 — The planning process: seven steps from setting objectives to follow-up, with the loop that feeds results back into the next plan.])

- *Step 1 — Setting objectives:* state exactly what is to be achieved — for
  the whole organisation and for every unit — because objectives are the
  destination every later step serves. Clear objectives make the rest of the
  process answerable.
- *Step 2 — Developing premises:* build the *assumptions about the future* on
  which plans will stand — demand, prices, technology, government policy. Every
  manager must work on the *same* premises, or departments will plan for
  different worlds.
- *Step 3 — Identifying alternatives:* list every sensible route to the
  objectives — expand capacity, outsource, licence a brand, combine two of
  these. The more honest the list, the better the final decision.
- *Step 4 — Evaluating alternatives:* weigh each option for cost, risk,
  feasibility and fit with the premises — positive and negative points,
  calculated rather than guessed.
- *Step 5 — Selecting the best alternative:* choose the option with the best
  balance of benefit and risk — or the best *combination* of options. This is
  the moment of decision.
- *Step 6 — Implementing the plan:* put the chosen plan into action — assign
  tasks, allocate budgets, buy materials, schedule the work. A plan on paper
  produces nothing.
- *Step 7 — Follow-up action:* compare actual results with the plan, find the
  deviations and correct the course — the bridge to controlling, and the
  source of lessons for the next plan.

