]

#analogy-box(title: "Everyday Example — Planning a College Fest")[
  You *set the objective* (a two-day fest for 2,000 visitors), *develop
  premises* (expected footfall, sponsor mood, monsoon dates), *list
  alternatives* (book the ground or the auditorium, one day or two),
  *evaluate* them for cost and rain risk, *select* the best, *implement* —
  committees, budgets, posters — and *follow up* after the fest to note what
  next year's committee should repeat. Every step of the process, one weekend
  of your life.
]

= Types of Plans

"Plan" is an umbrella word — under it sit eight distinct kinds, each answering
a different question. The exam's favourite task is to describe a plan in a
sentence and ask *which type it is*, so learn each type by its *question* and
its *example*. A further split runs across the list: *standing plans* are
reused again and again, while *single-use plans* are built for one situation
and retire when it ends.

== Standing plans — made once, used repeatedly

- *Objectives — the destinations:* the specific end-results a unit must
  achieve within a set time; everything else in the plan hierarchy serves
  them. *Example:* "increase sales by 10% this year".
- *Policy — the thinking guidelines:* general statements that guide managers'
  decisions and leave some room for judgment. *Example:* "prices are set
  within 10% of competitors' prices" or "no discount beyond 20% without
  approval".
- *Procedure — the sequence of steps:* the exact order in which a recurring
  task must be carried out; it tells you *which step comes next*. *Example:*
  the admission procedure — form, documents, fee, roll number.
- *Method — the way one step is done:* the prescribed manner of performing
  *one step* of a procedure; it tells you *how* that step is done. *Example:*
  teaching a topic by lecture or by activity — the procedure lists "teach",
  the method chooses how.
- *Rule — the no-discretion statement:* a specific statement of what must or
  must not be done, leaving *no room for judgment*. *Example:* "identity
  cards must be worn at all times" — no exceptions, no interpretation.

== Single-use plans — built for one situation

- *Strategy — the comprehensive game plan:* a broad plan that considers the
  *competitive situation and the environment* together and sets the direction
  for reaching the objective. *Example:* entering rural markets with
  low-cost small packs to beat a rival's urban strength.
- *Programme — the project package:* a *combination* of objectives, policies,
  procedures and budgets for one specific project, with its own sequence and
  deadlines. *Example:* the programme for launching a new product — every
  step from prototype to launch event.
- *Budget — the plan in numbers:* a statement of expected results *expressed
  in numerical terms* — rupees, units, hours — for a defined period. *Example:*
  "₹2 lakh for advertising this quarter"; the sales budget converts the sales
  objective into figures.

