= Common Errors and Special Cases

#exception-box(title: "Common Errors & Special Cases")[
  #table(
    columns: (1.35fr, 1.35fr, 1.2fr),
    table.header[*The mistake students make*][*What is right*][*The why / the trap*],
    ["A rule and a policy are the same"], [Rule = *no discretion*; policy = guideline that *allows judgment*], ["Identity cards at all times" allows no choice; "discounts up to 20%" leaves room to decide.],
    ["Procedure and method are the same"], [Procedure = the *sequence* of steps; method = the way *one step* is performed], [Admission procedure has steps; lecture-vs-activity is a method for the 'teach' step.],
    ["Budget and programme are standing plans"], [Both are *single-use* — built for one project or period], [A quarterly budget retires when the quarter ends; the standing plans keep serving.],
    ["Planning guarantees success"], [Planning *does not guarantee* success — it only prepares for it], [Plans still fail to a shifting environment or poor execution; the syllabus lists this as a limitation.],
    ["Planning comes after organising"], [Planning is the *primary* function — it precedes every other], [You cannot staff or direct around a plan that does not exist.],
    ["Developing premises means writing objectives"], [Premises are *assumptions about the future*, objectives are *goals*], ["Expected 15% demand growth" is a premise; "sell 10,000 units" is an objective.],
  )
]

- *Spot the pattern:* classification questions live on *discretion* — the
  less room the statement leaves for judgment, the closer it is to a rule.
- *One special case:* when a question describes "a combination of objectives,
  policies, procedures and rules for launching a project", the intended type
  is a *programme* — the package word is the giveaway.

